# Process rules

Shared working rules from the [harness](https://github.com/bodasooqa/harness) template. This file is managed: it is updated by `rootstock update` and is not edited by hand. The project writes its own rules in the root `AGENTS.md` (domain, stack, package boundaries, sources of truth, definitions) and in `.harness/local/`. `AGENTS.md` is read by all agents; `CLAUDE.md` next to it only includes it and this file for Claude Code.

## Language

All artifacts, code comments, doc comments, error messages, commit messages, PR texts, and the knowledge journal are written in English. Agents talk to the owner in {{ chat_language }}.

## Three places for documentation

The spec (`{{ spec_path }}`, revisions journal — `{{ revisions_path }}`) — requirements and the domain. `openspec/specs/` — implemented behavior by capability, derived from the spec. `knowledge/` — decisions with reasons, lessons, quirks of external APIs, open questions. Don't mix them; the "where to put it" rule — `knowledge/README.md` and the `knowledge-note` skill.

{% include 'template/_partials/snippets/spec-reading.md.jinja' %}

When in doubt about behavior — read the section, don't recall it.

The root `README.md` is not a fourth place but a showcase: what the system does, which stage it is at, how to run it, where things live. It must stay current and is edited in the same commit as what it describes: a package added or removed — the repository map; a run command or config key changed — the quick start.

## Workflow

Work goes through OpenSpec: propose → apply → archive (in Claude Code — `/opsx:propose`, `/opsx:apply`, `/opsx:archive`; in Pi — `/opsx-propose`, `/opsx-apply`, `/opsx-archive`). Capability and change names are kebab-case in English. Artifact rules and project context — in `openspec/config.yaml`.

One stage — several changes. A change name starts with the stage number: `stage3-<name>`. A stage is closed when all its changes are archived, the acceptance criterion from the spec's stages section is met (the `stage-acceptance` skill), and the stage table `{{ stages }}` shows it as `closed`. A change of the next stage does not start before that — and does not start before grooming of open questions (the `stage-grooming` skill, the list is printed by `knowledge/due.sh`).

A requirement that is not in the spec cannot be in a proposal. If something is missing — stop and ask.

The split with superpowers: **OpenSpec owns what is written down, superpowers owns how we work.**

- Before propose — `brainstorming`, so the proposal is not made up. Its terminal step ("invoke `writing-plans`") reads here as "move on to propose"; for the harness — as "lay out the plan in chat and record the decision in {% if doc_form == 'catalog' %}`{{ decisions_path }}/`{% else %}`{{ decisions_path }}`{% endif %}".
- On bugs — `systematic-debugging`. TDD is an executor rule, written in their prompts.
- From finished code to PR and cleanup after merge — the `change-finish` skill: full check run, reviewers, archiving, PR. It is the pre-completion check; `verification-before-completion`, `requesting-code-review`, `finishing-a-development-branch` are not invoked separately.
- `writing-plans` and `executing-plans` are not used: the plan lives in `openspec/changes/<name>/tasks.md`, we don't keep a second plan.
- Writing to `knowledge/` — the `knowledge-note` skill.

**The harness lives outside OpenSpec.** Skills, subagents, hooks, lefthook, CI, `knowledge/`, `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` do not go through OpenSpec: the spec says nothing about the harness, and a proposal for it is impossible under the process's own rules. The harness is edited directly, in its own worktree{% if linear %}, under its own Linear issue{% endif %}; decisions are recorded in {% if doc_form == 'catalog' %}`{{ decisions_path }}/`{% else %}`{{ decisions_path }}`{% endif %}.

### A worktree per session

A session that edits files works in a separate git worktree, not in the shared directory: the index and the working tree are shared per directory, and one session's commit carries away another's files. One worktree per change or per harness PR.

{% if orca %}
Worktrees are created by Orca. The main session is a curator in the main directory and edits no files there; a batch of edits goes to a child worktree with a supervised executor: `orca worktree create` → `orca orchestration worker-start` → `check` → acceptance → `orca worktree rm`. One task or batch of edits — one worktree, a child of the main directory: `orca worktree create --parent-worktree path:<main directory>`{% if linear %} with `--linear-issue <issue>`{% endif %}, without `--no-parent`. That way Orca shows whose work it is and which task it belongs to. Commands and pitfalls — the `orca-cli` and `orchestration` skills (Orca installs them itself). A worktree is not left without a running executor, and the empty terminal that `orca worktree create` opens by itself is closed after the executor starts. The directory the session started from is not removed: hooks resolve from it.
{% else %}
A worktree is created with `git worktree add ../<project>-<branch> -b <branch>` and removed with `git worktree remove` after merge (the `change-finish` skill). The directory the session started from is not removed: hooks resolve from it.
{% endif %}

Single-threaded work without other sessions may use the shared directory, but before a commit still run `git status` and stage files by name. The session start hook reports where the session works and which worktrees are taken; in the shared directory bulk staging (`git add -A`, `git add .`, `git commit -a`) is rejected by a hook.

### Don't edit generated files by hand

Only with the corresponding command:

{% if rust %}
- `target/**`, `Cargo.lock` — cargo;
{% endif %}
{% if frontend %}
- `node_modules/**`, `bun.lock` — bun;
{% endif %}
- `.claude/commands/opsx/**`, `.claude/skills/openspec-*/**`, `.pi/prompts/opsx-*`, `.pi/skills/openspec-*/**` — `openspec update`;
- blocks between `<!-- … -->` markers in `knowledge/` documents{% if doc_form == 'catalog' %} and in `{{ spec_path }}/README.md`{% endif %} — the `knowledge/` scripts;
- `.rootstock.toml` and managed template files — `rootstock update`;
- project generated files — per the "Sources of truth" table in the root `AGENTS.md`.

### Editing files

Edit files with the agent's edit tools (in Claude Code — Write and Edit, in Pi — `write` and `edit`), not with the shell. The harness hangs on edits: formatting, checks for the touched package, a procedure reminder when a contract is touched. An edit via `sed -i`, a heredoc or a redirect triggers none of this. The shell is for commands.

## Commands

Package managers — {% if rust %}`cargo` for Rust{% endif %}{% if rust and frontend %} and {% endif %}{% if frontend %}`bun` for JS{% endif %}. No wrappers or Makefile.{% if frontend and monorepo %} JS tasks are orchestrated by `turbo`{% if rust %}; cargo is not wrapped in turbo — it has its own graph and its own cache{% endif %}.{% endif %}


```
